{
  "format": "effect4-lcnf-roots-v3",
  "claim": "Hand-written recipe for the LCNF outputs, read by OCaml5.Lcnf.Export as a typed export plan. Each root of an artifact is in exactly one export class. A public root is a public promise: the artifact's interface exports it and the public facade re-exports it at its Lean path. A linkage root is called by hand code outside the artifact (the engine, the kit, a lane's test): the interface exports it and no facade does. An internal root is called only by the artifact's own prelude, and a coverage root is translated so that the route keeps covering its closure; the interface hides both. The plan checker refuses a root in two classes of one artifact, a root public in two artifacts, public roots in two artifacts and an entry key that is neither an option nor a class. The same Lean name in two artifacts is two specialised nodes and may take two classes, unless one artifact uses the other. The facade object names the public facade: its module, its file (GENERATED with its inferred interface), the committed manifest of every public name, and the manifest's version. The artifact that holds the public roots writes both. The manifest is append-only: at one version every committed path stays; a larger version declares a break. The producer stages every implementation, infers its interface from a fresh OCaml typed tree, and checks their agreement before publishing. The nominal list retains source package names with checked final ascriptions; each nominal root is exported. The abstract list hides whole package representations. An artifact may serve several consumers: each `consumers` entry names a facade file and the linkage roots it re-exports. The artifact is one walk of its own roots and then every consumer's roots, so the consumers share each node they reach. An artifact may compile its walk in scopes: `closure` names the unit that compiles every node its own roots reach, and `out` is then a facade over it whose interface keeps the public and linkage roots and seals the abstract packages; `tests` names the unit that compiles every node only the consumers reach, which includes the closure. Each consumer's facade includes the tests unit and keeps its consumer's roots and the types they name. So each node is compiled once. The four test lanes are the consumers of ocaml/gen/api_gen.ml: the closure ocaml/gen/closure_gen.ml is a package-private library of effect4_gen, and the tests unit ocaml/engine/test/lanes/lanes_gen.ml is a test library that nothing installs. The first artifact owns the program types. Later artifacts open that owner. The second lowers the carriers' images that the program library's byte codec (ocaml/eff/eff_codec.ml) composes with the framing kernel, so the library and the engine link no api closure. An artifact may name the earlier artifacts it uses (`uses`, each by its module and its artifact, as `open` names the owner). Its walk stops at every declaration that a used artifact's walk lowers and writes that artifact's binding by its module, and it spells that artifact's own types through it. A root it lists that a used artifact lowers is bound by name, and the used artifact's interface exports what its users link. So a Lean declaration is lowered into one artifact: the api artifact uses the second and lowers no node of the program codec. A used artifact is one flat walk of its own roots, over its user's owner and within its user's cap; the plan checker refuses any other use. Regenerate with python3 scripts/generate.py --only lcnf.",
